</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>École Polytechnique
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Route de Saclay, 91120 Palaiseau, France
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">alain.durmus@polytechnique.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper addresses the problem of inverse covariance (also known as precision matrix) estimation in
high-dimensional settings. Specifically, we focus on two classes of estimators: linear shrinkage estimators
with a target proportional to the identity matrix, and estimators derived from data augmentation (DA).
Here, DA refers to the common practice of enriching a dataset with artificial samples—typically generated via
a generative model or through random transformations of the original data—prior to model fitting.
For both classes of estimators, we derive estimators and provide concentration bounds for their quadratic error.
This allows for both method comparison and hyperparameter tuning, such as selecting the optimal proportion of
artificial samples. On the technical side, our analysis relies on tools from random matrix theory.
We introduce a novel deterministic equivalent for generalized resolvent matrices, accommodating dependent
samples with specific structure. We support our theoretical results with numerical experiments.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id4" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">A Preprint
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
<table id="p1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we consider the problem of estimating the inverse covariance matrix,
also known as the precision matrix, of a random vector from i.i.d. zero-mean samples <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X_{1},\cdots,X_{n}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X_{1},\cdots,X_{n}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> with true covariance <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{X}=\mathbb{E}\left[X_{1}X_{1}^{\top}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{X}=\mathbb{E}\left[X_{1}X_{1}^{\top}\right]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of samples, and <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimensionality of the data. This problem has important applications in statistics and signal processing (see, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al. (2016)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlson (1988)</a></cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We are particularly interested in the high-dimensional regimes, where the data dimension <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and the number of samples <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> are of the same order. In this setting, the sample covariance matrix <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{X}=n^{-1}XX^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{X}=n^{-1}XX^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> may be non-invertible or poorly-conditionned. As a result, using its inverse as an estimator can lead to numerical instability and high estimation error.
To address this issue, shrinkage estimators for the covariance matrix have been proposed as a regularization method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bodnar et al. (2016)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ledoit &amp; Wolf (2004)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schafer &amp; Strimmer (2005)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2003)</a></cite>, which involve adding a target matrix to <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. The simplest and most common choice for the target is a multiple of the identity matrix, which effectively shifts the eigenvalues above a threshold <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, improving stability.
In addition to linear shrinkage and even more importantly, this paper also explores the use of data augmentation (DA) as an alternative strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Data augmentation (DA) involves increasing the size of a dataset by incorporating additional artificial samples. The underlying intuition is that, in many cases, it is possible to artificially replicate the data distribution, thereby reducing the variance of the model while maintaining relatively low bias. Due to its effectiveness in low-data regimes and its ability to mitigate overfitting,
DA has become increasingly popular and is now widely used in machine learning and data science <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shorten &amp; Khoshgoftaar (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gidaris et al. (2018)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2020b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grill et al. (2020)</a></cite>.
It finds applications across a variety of fields, including computer vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shorten &amp; Khoshgoftaar (2019)</a></cite>, natural language processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al. (2021)</a></cite>, and neuroscience <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lashgari et al. (2020)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Two main types of data augmentation (DA) can be distinguished.
The first is Transformative Data Augmentation (TDA), where original samples are transformed through a random mapping—for example, by adding Gaussian noise or applying a random mask.
The second is Generative Data Augmentation (GDA), in which artificial samples are generated using a generative model and added to the training dataset.
In the case of GDA, we assume that the generative model has been pre-trained on the original samples <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In both cases, the artificial samples are dependent on the original data.
Although TDA and GDA differ conceptually, our framework and results encompass both approaches.
More precisely, we consider the inverse of the empirical covariance matrix associated with the augmented dataset <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X}=[X_{1},\ldots,X_{n},G_{1},\ldots,G_{m}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X}=[X_{1},\ldots,X_{n},G_{1},\ldots,G_{m}]</annotation></semantics></math> as an estimator of <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{X}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{X}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is an artificial sample.
That is, we study the estimator which consists of the inverse of <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n+m)^{-1}\tilde{X}\tilde{X}^{\top}+\lambda\operatorname{I}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n+m)^{-1}\tilde{X}\tilde{X}^{\top}+\lambda\operatorname{I}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, for some regularization parameter <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Although there is extensive empirical evidence that DA improves the performance of machine learning models, the theoretical literature on the subject remains relatively limited.
In this work, our goal is to establish performance guarantees that enable meaningful comparisons between different DA strategies and, as a by-product, allow for the optimization of certain associated hyperparameters. To this end, we leverage tools from random matrix theory to construct estimators of the quadratic error for DA-based estimators, which, under mild assumptions, satisfy exponential concentration inequalities.
Our analysis can also be adapted—and in fact simplifies—to cover linear shrinkage estimators with a target proportional to the identity matrix.
To summarize, our main contributions are as follows:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:15pt;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I1.i1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S2" title="2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we focus on the estimator of <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{X}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{X}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
given by the inverse of a linear shrinkage estimator, where
the shrinkage target is a scalar multiple of the identity
matrix. Specifically, we derive estimators for the
quadratic error and show that they satisfy non-asymptotic
exponential concentration bounds. Our results hold under
standard assumptions from random matrix theory—namely, the
Lipschitz concentration property for <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I1.i2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S3" title="3 Precision matrix estimation using generic data augmentation ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we extend our analysis to data-augmented
estimators under appropriate conditions on the
DA procedure, which we show hold true for common DA.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I1.i3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, for both scenarios, we show how our estimators for the quadratic error can be used to compare and tune their corresponding methods with respect to key hyper-parameters such as the nomber of additional samples <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> for DA. These conclusions are illustrated numerically on real data in <a href="#S4" title="4 Numerical experiments ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Notation and convention.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by high-dimensional statistics, we will consider that <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> are variables, yet for notation simplicity, we will most often not reflect dependancies in <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> in our notations. Additionally, we write, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\lesssim y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≲</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\lesssim y</annotation></semantics></math> (resp <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\gtrsim y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≳</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\gtrsim y</annotation></semantics></math>) whenever <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\leq Cy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\leq Cy</annotation></semantics></math> (resp <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\geq Cy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\geq Cy</annotation></semantics></math>) for a universal constant <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> that neither depends on the model’s parameters, nor on the parameters <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any matrix <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we denoted by <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathbf{H}}=k^{-1}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{H}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝐇</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐇𝐇</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathbf{H}}=k^{-1}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{H}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding covariance matrix. For any symmetric matrix <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uplambda_{d}(\Sigma)\leq\ldots\leq\uplambda_{1}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">λ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uplambda_{d}(\Sigma)\leq\ldots\leq\uplambda_{1}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> its eigenvalues. The Frobenius and operator norms are denoted <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\cdot\right\|_{\mathrm{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\cdot\right\|_{\mathrm{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\cdot\right\|_{\mathrm{op}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>op</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\cdot\right\|_{\mathrm{op}}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively.
Random variables will be referred to by capital letters <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, and we will denote by <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Ber}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ber</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Ber}(p)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}(\mathbf{m},\mathbf{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}(\mathbf{m},\mathbf{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Unif}(\mathsf{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖤</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Unif}(\mathsf{E})</annotation></semantics></math> the Bernoulli distribution of parameter <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>,
the Gaussian distribution of mean <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and covariance <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Sigma}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Sigma}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the uniform distribution over a discrete set <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{E}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, we introduce the <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-Wasserstein metric, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{p}^{p}(\nu_{1},\nu_{2})=\operatorname{inf}_{\gamma\in\Gamma(\nu_{1},\nu_{2})}\int\left\|x-y\right\|_{\mathrm{F}}^{p}\;\mathrm{d}\gamma(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>inf</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mo>∫</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{p}^{p}(\nu_{1},\nu_{2})=\operatorname{inf}_{\gamma\in\Gamma(\nu_{1},\nu_{2})}\int\left\|x-y\right\|_{\mathrm{F}}^{p}\;\mathrm{d}\gamma(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> for any distributions <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and where <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in\Gamma(\nu_{1},\nu_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in\Gamma(\nu_{1},\nu_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if for any <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{E}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖤</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{E}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(\mathbb{R}^{d\times n},\mathsf{E})=\nu_{1}(\mathsf{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝖤</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖤</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(\mathbb{R}^{d\times n},\mathsf{E})=\nu_{1}(\mathsf{E})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(\mathsf{E},\mathbb{R}^{d\times n})=\nu_{2}(\mathsf{E})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖤</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖤</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(\mathsf{E},\mathbb{R}^{d\times n})=\nu_{2}(\mathsf{E}).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Related work</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data Augmentation.</span>
Numerous empirical studies have demonstrated the benefits of using DA when training machine learning models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mumuni &amp; Mumuni (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maharana et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">van Dyk &amp; Meng (2001)</a></cite>.
Among popular DA schemes, we can mention AutoAugment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cubuk et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lim et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2020)</a></cite>, which aims to learn an optimal augmentation policy from data by combining a set of sub-policies. In addition, significant works have also explored the incorporation of knowledge about the distribution invariances directly into the training procedures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2020a)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, DA does not always lead to a systematic improvement in test error <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirichenko et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hernandez-Garcia &amp; Konig (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cetingoz &amp; Lehalle (2025)</a></cite>,
and very little theoretical understanding backs-up the improvement observed empirically.
An early analysis by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bishop (1995)</a></cite> showed that adding Gaussian noise to data points is equivalent to applying Tikhonov regularization. Building on this seminal work—and given the practical importance of DA for machine learning practitioners—a few studies have sought to develop a theoretical understanding of its effects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of kernel methods, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao et al. (2019)</a></cite> showed that DA can be approximated by a combination of first-order feature averaging and second-order variance regularization. Similarly, in the context of linear and logistic regressions, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2022)</a></cite> revealed that DA induces implicit spectral regularization in two ways: first, by adjusting the relative proportions of the eigenvalues of the data covariance matrix in a training-dependent way; and second, by uniformly shifting the entire spectrum via ridge regression.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Taking a different perspective, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2020)</a></cite> consider a family of linear transformations and study their effects on the ridge estimator in an over-parametrized linear regression setting. First, they show that transformations that preserve the labels of the data can improve estimation by increasing the span of the training data. Second, they show that transformations that mix data can improve estimation by playing a regularization effect. They proposed an augmentation scheme that searches over the linear span of a set of transformations, aiming to maximize model uncertainty on the transformed data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, studies have shown that even small amounts of artificial data can lead to <span id="S1.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">model collapse</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shumailov et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dohmatob et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dohmatob et al. (2025)</a></cite>, a phenomenon where the performance of generative models deteriorates when recursively trained on synthetic data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Precision Matrix Estimation.</span>
In a high-dimensional settings—where the number of covariates is comparable to or exceeds the number of observations—traditional covariance estimation methods often suffer from poor conditioning, making the estimation of the precision matrix particularly challenging. To address this, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ledoit &amp; Wolf (2004)</a></cite> introduced linear shrinkage methods, which involve forming a convex combination <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varpi C_{X}+(1-\varpi)\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϖ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐓</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varpi C_{X}+(1-\varpi)\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> of the sample covariance matrix <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> with a shrinkage target <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varpi\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varpi\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ledoit &amp; Wolf (2003)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ledoit &amp; Wolf (2004)</a></cite> derived the optimal value of <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varpi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϖ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varpi</annotation></semantics></math> that minimizes the mean squared error between the shrinkage estimator and the true covariance matrix <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Extending this work, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ledoit &amp; Wolf (2012)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ledoit &amp; Wolf (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benaych-Georges et al. (2023)</a></cite> proposed and analyzed non-linear shrinkage estimators of the form <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Uf(D)U^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Uf(D)U^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{X}=UDU^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{X}=UDU^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> is the eigenvalue decomposition of <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a suitably chosen function applied to the eigenvalues.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Fewer works have addressed shrinkage methods specifically designed for precision matrix estimation. Among them, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bodnar et al. (2016)</a></cite> studied estimators of the form <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\varpi)C_{X}^{-1}+\varpi\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϖ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\varpi)C_{X}^{-1}+\varpi\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> is a deterministic shrinkage target, and derived the optimal shrinkage intensity <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varpi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϖ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varpi</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2015)</a></cite> considered estimators of the form <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{X}(\varpi)=((1-\varpi)C_{X}+\varpi\operatorname{I}_{d})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϖ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϖ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{X}(\varpi)=((1-\varpi)C_{X}+\varpi\operatorname{I}_{d})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and derived the optimal <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varpi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϖ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varpi</annotation></semantics></math> to minimize the objective function <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\Omega_{X}(\varpi)\Sigma_{X}-\operatorname{I}_{d}\right\|_{\mathrm{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϖ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\Omega_{X}(\varpi)\Sigma_{X}-\operatorname{I}_{d}\right\|_{\mathrm{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> in the high-dimensional regime where <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d/n\to\gamma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d/n\to\gamma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">An independent line of research, motivated by Gaussian graphical models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan &amp; Lin (2007)</a></cite>, has focused on estimating sparse precision matrices. In particular, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazumder &amp; Hastie (2012)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al. (2011)</a></cite> introduced the Graphical Lasso method, which has become widely used for this purpose.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Random Matrix Theory.</span>
Since the pioneering work of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wishart (1928)</a></cite>, numerous studies have investigated the behavior of the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the sample covariance matrix <math id="S1.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> in the high-dimensional regime where <math id="S1.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d/n\rightarrow\gamma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d/n\rightarrow\gamma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>; see, for example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marchenko &amp; Pastur (1967)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Silverstein (1989)</a></cite>.
More recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alex et al. (2014)</a></cite> first demonstrated that, in the isotropic setting, the resolvent <math id="S1.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{X}+\lambda\operatorname{I}_{d})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{X}+\lambda\operatorname{I}_{d})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> converges weakly to a scalar multiple of the identity matrix as <math id="S1.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d/n\rightarrow\gamma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d/n\rightarrow\gamma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. This was later extended to the anisotropic case by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Knowles &amp; Yin (2017)</a></cite>, who established so-called deterministic equivalent results for <math id="S1.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{X}+\lambda\operatorname{I}_{d})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{X}+\lambda\operatorname{I}_{d})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
These results have been further generalized to settings with more complex dependency structures. In particular, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chouard (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Louart &amp; Couillet (2023)</a></cite> showed that deterministic equivalents continue to hold under weaker assumptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Building on these foundational results, several studies have established connections between classical random matrix theory—particularly the results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marchenko &amp; Pastur (1967)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Silverstein (1989)</a></cite>—and the behavior of modern machine learning models. In particular, random matrix theory has proven instrumental in explaining the <span id="S1.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">double-descent</span> phenomenon, initially observed in linear models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hastie et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Derezinski et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muthukumar et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bartlett et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al. (2021)</a></cite>, and later extended to certain classes of shallow models, such as random feature models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mei &amp; Montanari (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liao et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gerace et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">D’Ascoli et al. (2020)</a></cite>.
Complementing these works, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schroder et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schroder et al. (2024a)</a></cite> provided a sharp asymptotic characterization of the test error in deep random feature models—representing a significant step toward understanding generalization in deeper architectures. Finally, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ilbert et al. (2024)</a></cite> leveraged random matrix theory to develop precise performance estimates for multi-task learning across a variety of statistical models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider here the following estimator <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> of the precision matrix, and its squared error:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)=(C_{X}+\lambda\operatorname{I}_{d})^{-1}\;,\qquad\mathcal{E}_{X}(\lambda):=\frac{1}{d}\|R_{X}(\lambda)-\Sigma_{X}^{-1}\|_{\mathrm{F}}^{2}\;." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)=(C_{X}+\lambda\operatorname{I}_{d})^{-1}\;,\qquad\mathcal{E}_{X}(\lambda):=\frac{1}{d}\|R_{X}(\lambda)-\Sigma_{X}^{-1}\|_{\mathrm{F}}^{2}\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter that controls the strength of the regularization. Note that this estimator is not per se a shrinkage estimator, but it is the inverse of a shrinkage estimator of the covariance matrix. In addition, <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is also referred to as the diagonal loading estimator in the signal processing community; see e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2003)</a></cite>. Furthermore, note that our result can be readily applied to an estimators of the form <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((1-\alpha)C_{X}+\alpha\sigma\operatorname{I}_{d})^{-1}=(1-\alpha)^{-1}(C_{X}+\alpha\sigma\operatorname{I}_{d}/(1-\alpha))^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((1-\alpha)C_{X}+\alpha\sigma\operatorname{I}_{d})^{-1}=(1-\alpha)^{-1}(C_{X}+\alpha\sigma\operatorname{I}_{d}/(1-\alpha))^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\left(0,1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\left(0,1\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This estimator does not rely on any data augmentation procedure. However, as we will see, applying data augmentation leads to results that are closely related to this regularization approach. This is not surprising, as it is relatively well known that data augmentation induces an implicit regularization effect <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bishop (1995)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2022)</a></cite>. For this reason, we present this simple case in detail as a preliminary step, which will allow us to transition more smoothly to the data-augmented case in <a href="#S3" title="3 Precision matrix estimation using generic data augmentation ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
As already emphasized in the introduction, our main goal is to derive a data-centric estimate for the error <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{X}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{X}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>. To this end, we introduce two assumptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="Thmassumption1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Concentration of <math id="Thmassumption1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math></span>)<span id="Thmassumption1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The random matrix <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> writes <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{1/2}Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{1/2}Z</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> has independant sub-Gaussian entries with parameter <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#Thmassumption1" title="H 1 (Concentration of 𝑋). ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is standard in the random matrix theory literature, yet one can employ a more general framework, as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chouard (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ilbert et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Louart &amp; Couillet (2018)</a></cite>
by introducing the notion of Lipschitz concentration (<a href="#Thmassumption6" title="H 6 (Lipschitz concentration of the data). ‣ A.2 Discussions on , , ‣ Appendix A In-depth justification of the hypothesis ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>),
we detail this generalization throughout the appendix and stick to this simpler framework in the main body for the sake of simplicity.
In addition, we expect our results to remain valid under a finite-moment assumption on the entries of <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, albeit with weaker—typically polynomial—concentration bounds, as done in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ledoit &amp; Peche (2011)</a></cite>.
We leave a detailed investigation of this extension for future work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We next suppose that with high probability the leave one-out covariance matrix <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{X}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{X}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> is well-conditionned. This matrix is defined for any <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> as
the covariance matrix</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathbf{X}}^{-}=C_{\mathbf{X}^{-}}\;,\qquad\mathbf{X}^{-}=[0,\mathbf{X}_{2},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{n}]\;." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathbf{X}}^{-}=C_{\mathbf{X}^{-}}\;,\qquad\mathbf{X}^{-}=[0,\mathbf{X}_{2},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{n}]\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">More formally, for any <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> define</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{A}_{\eta}=\{\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}:\uplambda_{d}(C_{\mathbf{X}}^{-})\geq\eta\}\;." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">λ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{A}_{\eta}=\{\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}:\uplambda_{d}(C_{\mathbf{X}}^{-})\geq\eta\}\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="Thmassumption2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model conditionning</span>)<span id="Thmassumption2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{X}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{X}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\left(X\notin\mathsf{A}_{\eta}\right)\lesssim\mathrm{e}^{-c_{X}n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\left(X\notin\mathsf{A}_{\eta}\right)\lesssim\mathrm{e}^{-c_{X}n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We highlight in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A In-depth justification of the hypothesis ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> that <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="H 2 (Model conditionning). ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> holds provided <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="H 1 (Concentration of 𝑋). ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> holds and <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq K_{X}(d+\eta+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq K_{X}(d+\eta+1)</annotation></semantics></math> for some constant <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> depending only on <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We are now ready to present our estimator for <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{X}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{X}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> and to state its concentration properties. The estimator is given by:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textstyle\hat{\mathcal{E}}_{X}(\lambda):=\dfrac{1}{d}\left(\operatorname{tr}\left(\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)^{2}\right)-\dfrac{2(1-d/n)\operatorname{tr}\left(\operatorname{R}_{X}(0)\right)}{\lambda}\mathbbm{1}_{\mathsf{A}_{\eta}}(X)+\dfrac{2tr\left(\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)}{\lambda\mathfrak{b}(\lambda)}+\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}^{-2}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔟</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textstyle\hat{\mathcal{E}}_{X}(\lambda):=\dfrac{1}{d}\left(\operatorname{tr}\left(\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)^{2}\right)-\dfrac{2(1-d/n)\operatorname{tr}\left(\operatorname{R}_{X}(0)\right)}{\lambda}\mathbbm{1}_{\mathsf{A}_{\eta}}(X)+\dfrac{2tr\left(\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)}{\lambda\mathfrak{b}(\lambda)}+\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}^{-2}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathfrak{b}(\lambda):=\dfrac{1}{1-d/n+(\lambda/n)\operatorname{tr}\left(\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)}\;." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathfrak{b}(\lambda):=\dfrac{1}{1-d/n+(\lambda/n)\operatorname{tr}\left(\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)}\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Note that for a fixed <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{E}}_{X}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{E}}_{X}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is computable from the data <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> only, up to an additive constant.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="H 1 (Concentration of 𝑋). ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="H 2 (Model conditionning). ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Then, it holds for all <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\left(\left|\mathcal{E}_{X}(\lambda)-\hat{\mathcal{E}}_{X}(\lambda)\right|\geq t+\Delta_{X}(\lambda)\right)\lesssim\exp\left(-c\uplambda_{d}(\Sigma_{X})^{2}\sigma_{X}^{2}nd\eta^{3}t^{2}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">λ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\left(\left|\mathcal{E}_{X}(\lambda)-\hat{\mathcal{E}}_{X}(\lambda)\right|\geq t+\Delta_{X}(\lambda)\right)\lesssim\exp\left(-c\uplambda_{d}(\Sigma_{X})^{2}\sigma_{X}^{2}nd\eta^{3}t^{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for a universal constant <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and where</span></p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{X}(\lambda):=\dfrac{C_{1}\sigma_{X}^{2}\sqrt{d}\|\Sigma_{X}\|_{\mathrm{op}}^{3}}{n\uplambda_{d}(\Sigma_{X})\eta^{6}}+C_{2}\mathrm{e}^{-c_{X}n}+\dfrac{1}{\lambda^{3}nd}\;." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>op</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">λ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{X}(\lambda):=\dfrac{C_{1}\sigma_{X}^{2}\sqrt{d}\|\Sigma_{X}\|_{\mathrm{op}}^{3}}{n\uplambda_{d}(\Sigma_{X})\eta^{6}}+C_{2}\mathrm{e}^{-c_{X}n}+\dfrac{1}{\lambda^{3}nd}\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},C_{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},C_{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> are explicit polynomial functions of <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Sigma_{X}\|_{\mathrm{op}}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>op</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Sigma_{X}\|_{\mathrm{op}}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uplambda_{d}(\Sigma_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">λ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uplambda_{d}(\Sigma_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta+\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta+\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{X}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{X}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, see (<a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Proof of ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">From a practical standpoint, the previous result can be used to optimize the hyperparameter <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> by minimizing the function <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\mapsto\mathcal{E}_{X}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\mapsto\mathcal{E}_{X}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, using <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{E}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{E}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> as a proxy. Moreover, it is worth noting that the derivative of <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\mapsto\hat{\mathcal{E}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\mapsto\hat{\mathcal{E}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on the data matrix <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and not on the true covariance <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. As a result, <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{E}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{E}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> can be minimized using a gradient descent scheme, provided that the parameter <math id="S2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="H 2 (Model conditionning). ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. We illustrate these applications on real data in <a href="#S4" title="4 Numerical experiments ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Although the full proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is deferred to <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Proof of ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, we provide here a sketch of its derivation. We highlight the main ideas and technical challenges, and note that the proof of our result on estimation using data augmentation, <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 3 Precision matrix estimation using generic data augmentation ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, shares several of these steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">First, expanding the Frobenius norm in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), we have,</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{X}(\lambda)=(1/d)\operatorname{tr}\left(\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)^{2}\right)-(2/d)\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}^{-1}\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)+(1/d)\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}^{-2}\right)\;." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{X}(\lambda)=(1/d)\operatorname{tr}\left(\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)^{2}\right)-(2/d)\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}^{-1}\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)+(1/d)\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}^{-2}\right)\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p9.2" class="ltx_p">The first term in the previous expansion is directly computable from the data matrix <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, while the last term is constant with respect to <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and can be ignored when the goal is to optimize <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, it suffices to establish a deterministic equivalent for <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> and thus of <math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}^{-1}\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}^{-1}\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. To this end, we rely on the following result, whose proof is postponed to <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Proof of ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="H 1 (Concentration of 𝑋). ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="H 2 (Model conditionning). ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Let <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> be a deterministic matrix, then we have for all <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\left(\left|\dfrac{1}{d}\operatorname{tr}\left(\mathbf{B}\left\{\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\mathbbm{1}_{\mathsf{A}_{\eta}}(X)-\mathbb{E}\left[\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\mathbbm{1}_{\mathsf{A}_{\eta}}(X)\right]\right\}\right)\right|\geq t\right)\lesssim\exp\left(-c(\eta+\lambda)^{3}\sigma_{X}^{2}ndt^{2}\right)\;." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\left(\left|\dfrac{1}{d}\operatorname{tr}\left(\mathbf{B}\left\{\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\mathbbm{1}_{\mathsf{A}_{\eta}}(X)-\mathbb{E}\left[\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\mathbbm{1}_{\mathsf{A}_{\eta}}(X)\right]\right\}\right)\right|\geq t\right)\lesssim\exp\left(-c(\eta+\lambda)^{3}\sigma_{X}^{2}ndt^{2}\right)\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore, defining <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\lambda}(\mathfrak{b})=1+n^{-1}\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}(\Sigma_{X}/\mathfrak{b}+\lambda\operatorname{I}_{d})^{-1}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>𝔟</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\lambda}(\mathfrak{b})=1+n^{-1}\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}(\Sigma_{X}/\mathfrak{b}+\lambda\operatorname{I}_{d})^{-1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{b}^{*}:=\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{b}^{*}:=\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> as the unique fixed point of <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|\mathbb{E}\left[\left\{\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)-\bar{\operatorname{R}}_{X}^{\mathfrak{b}^{*}}(\lambda)\right\}\mathbbm{1}_{\mathsf{A}_{\eta}}(X)\right]\right\|_{\mathrm{F}}\lesssim\dfrac{C_{1}\sigma_{X}^{2}\sqrt{d}\|\Sigma_{X}\|_{\mathrm{op}}^{3}}{n\uplambda_{1}(\Sigma_{X})(\eta+\lambda)^{6}}+C_{2}\mathrm{e}^{-c_{X}n}\;," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>op</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|\mathbb{E}\left[\left\{\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)-\bar{\operatorname{R}}_{X}^{\mathfrak{b}^{*}}(\lambda)\right\}\mathbbm{1}_{\mathsf{A}_{\eta}}(X)\right]\right\|_{\mathrm{F}}\lesssim\dfrac{C_{1}\sigma_{X}^{2}\sqrt{d}\|\Sigma_{X}\|_{\mathrm{op}}^{3}}{n\uplambda_{1}(\Sigma_{X})(\eta+\lambda)^{6}}+C_{2}\mathrm{e}^{-c_{X}n}\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{\operatorname{R}}_{X}^{\mathfrak{b}^{*}}(\lambda)=\left(\dfrac{\Sigma_{X}}{\mathfrak{b}^{*}}+\lambda\operatorname{I}_{d}\right)^{-1}\;," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{\operatorname{R}}_{X}^{\mathfrak{b}^{*}}(\lambda)=\left(\dfrac{\Sigma_{X}}{\mathfrak{b}^{*}}+\lambda\operatorname{I}_{d}\right)^{-1}\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined as in <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> concentrates around <math id="S2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\operatorname{R}}_{X}^{\mathfrak{b}^{*}}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\operatorname{R}}_{X}^{\mathfrak{b}^{*}}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, which we refer to as a deterministic equivalent of <math id="S2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>. Our result extends that of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chouard (2022)</a></cite> by covering the case of vanishing regularization (<math id="S2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>). This extension constitutes the main technical innovation required for the proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We can now leverage the deterministic equivalent of <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> to rewrite <math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/d)\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}^{-1}\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/d)\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}^{-1}\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Informally, it holds with high probability that</p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{d}\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}^{-1}\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{d}\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}^{-1}\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx\dfrac{1}{d}\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}^{-1}\left(\dfrac{\Sigma_{X}}{\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)}+\lambda\operatorname{I}_{d}\right)^{-1}\right)\mathbbm{1}_{\mathsf{A}_{\eta}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx\dfrac{1}{d}\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}^{-1}\left(\dfrac{\Sigma_{X}}{\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)}+\lambda\operatorname{I}_{d}\right)^{-1}\right)\mathbbm{1}_{\mathsf{A}_{\eta}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\dfrac{1}{\lambda d\mathfrak{b}^{*}(0)}\operatorname{tr}\left(\bar{\operatorname{R}}_{X}^{\mathfrak{b}^{*}(0)}(0)\right)\mathbbm{1}_{\mathsf{A}_{\eta}}(X)-\dfrac{1}{\lambda{d}\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)}\operatorname{tr}\left(\bar{\operatorname{R}}_{X}^{\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)}(\lambda)\right)\mathbbm{1}_{\mathsf{A}_{\eta}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\dfrac{1}{\lambda d\mathfrak{b}^{*}(0)}\operatorname{tr}\left(\bar{\operatorname{R}}_{X}^{\mathfrak{b}^{*}(0)}(0)\right)\mathbbm{1}_{\mathsf{A}_{\eta}}(X)-\dfrac{1}{\lambda{d}\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)}\operatorname{tr}\left(\bar{\operatorname{R}}_{X}^{\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)}(\lambda)\right)\mathbbm{1}_{\mathsf{A}_{\eta}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p11.2" class="ltx_p">where the last equality follows from the identity <math id="S2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}^{-1}-\mathbf{B}^{-1}=\mathbf{A}^{-1}\{\mathbf{B}-\mathbf{A}\}\mathbf{B}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐀</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}^{-1}-\mathbf{B}^{-1}=\mathbf{A}^{-1}\{\mathbf{B}-\mathbf{A}\}\mathbf{B}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, using <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> again, and that <math id="S2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{b}^{*}(0)=(1-d/n)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{b}^{*}(0)=(1-d/n)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the fixed point of <math id="S2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}:\mathfrak{b}\mapsto 1+\mathfrak{b}d/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝔟</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔟</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}:\mathfrak{b}\mapsto 1+\mathfrak{b}d/n</annotation></semantics></math>, we get</p>
<table id="S2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dfrac{1}{d}\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}^{-1}\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)\approx\dfrac{1-d/n}{\lambda d}\operatorname{tr}\left(\operatorname{R}_{X}(0)\right)\mathbbm{1}_{\mathsf{A}_{\eta}}(X)-\dfrac{1}{\lambda d\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)}\operatorname{tr}\left(\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)\;." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dfrac{1}{d}\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}^{-1}\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)\approx\dfrac{1-d/n}{\lambda d}\operatorname{tr}\left(\operatorname{R}_{X}(0)\right)\mathbbm{1}_{\mathsf{A}_{\eta}}(X)-\dfrac{1}{\lambda d\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)}\operatorname{tr}\left(\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p11.3" class="ltx_p">Finally, by the definition of <math id="S2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> and through straightforward algebraic manipulations, we obtain</p>
<table id="S2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=1+\dfrac{1}{n}\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}\bar{\operatorname{R}}_{X}^{\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)}(\lambda)\right)=1+\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)\left\{\dfrac{d}{n}-\dfrac{\lambda}{n}\operatorname{tr}\left(\bar{\operatorname{R}}_{X}^{\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)}(\lambda)\right)\right\}\;." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=1+\dfrac{1}{n}\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{X}\bar{\operatorname{R}}_{X}^{\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)}(\lambda)\right)=1+\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)\left\{\dfrac{d}{n}-\dfrac{\lambda}{n}\operatorname{tr}\left(\bar{\operatorname{R}}_{X}^{\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)}(\lambda)\right)\right\}\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p11.4" class="ltx_p">Therefore, applying <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> again yields</p>
<table id="S2.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)=\dfrac{1}{1-(d/n)+(\lambda/n)\operatorname{tr}\left(\bar{\operatorname{R}}_{X}^{\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)}(\lambda)\right)}\approx\dfrac{1}{1-(d/n)+(\lambda/n)\operatorname{tr}\left(\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)}=\mathfrak{b}(\lambda)\;," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)=\dfrac{1}{1-(d/n)+(\lambda/n)\operatorname{tr}\left(\bar{\operatorname{R}}_{X}^{\mathfrak{b}^{*}(\lambda)}(\lambda)\right)}\approx\dfrac{1}{1-(d/n)+(\lambda/n)\operatorname{tr}\left(\operatorname{R}_{X}(\lambda)\right)}=\mathfrak{b}(\lambda)\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p11.5" class="ltx_p">Plugging this estimate in (<a href="#S2.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), we identify <math id="S2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{E}}_{X}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{E}}_{X}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) and it completes the proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The formal proof is postponed to <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Proof of ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> in the supplement.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Precision matrix estimation using generic data augmentation</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we investigate a data augmentation strategy to improve the estimation of the inverse covariance matrix of <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, we consider an additional set of artificial samples <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=[G_{1},\cdots,G_{m}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=[G_{1},\cdots,G_{m}]</annotation></semantics></math>, which may depend on <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and are typically generated using either TDA or GDA techniques; see <a href="#S3.T1" title="In 3 Precision matrix estimation using generic data augmentation ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
More precisely, given <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, we assume that <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn from a known regular conditional distribution <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{X},\mathsf{A})\mapsto\nu_{\mathbf{X}}(\mathsf{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝖠</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>𝐗</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖠</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{X},\mathsf{A})\mapsto\nu_{\mathbf{X}}(\mathsf{A})</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that for any measurable set <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{E}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖤</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{E}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we have <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\left(G_{i}\in\mathsf{E}\mid X\right)=\nu_{X}(\mathsf{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝖤</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖤</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\left(G_{i}\in\mathsf{E}\mid X\right)=\nu_{X}(\mathsf{E})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, we consider the following new estimator and define its quadratic error as:</p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{R}_{\mathrm{Aug}}(\lambda):=\left((n+m)^{-1}\{XX^{\top}+GG^{\top}\}+\lambda\operatorname{I}_{d}\right)^{-1}\;,\quad\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{Aug}}(\lambda):=(1/d)\|\operatorname{R}_{\mathrm{Aug}}(\lambda)-\Sigma_{X}^{-1}\|_{\mathrm{F}}^{2}\;." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>Aug</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>Aug</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>Aug</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{R}_{\mathrm{Aug}}(\lambda):=\left((n+m)^{-1}\{XX^{\top}+GG^{\top}\}+\lambda\operatorname{I}_{d}\right)^{-1}\;,\quad\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{Aug}}(\lambda):=(1/d)\|\operatorname{R}_{\mathrm{Aug}}(\lambda)-\Sigma_{X}^{-1}\|_{\mathrm{F}}^{2}\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In the following, in addition to <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="H 1 (Concentration of 𝑋). ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="H 2 (Model conditionning). ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, which pertain to <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, we introduce further assumptions on <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. These are organized into three categories: a concentration assumption on <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, a smoothness assumption on <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a stability assumption on <math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H 3</span></span><span id="Thmassumption3.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="Thmassumption3.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Concentration of <math id="Thmassumption3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math></span>)<span id="Thmassumption3.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmassumption3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The random matrix <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> has i.i.d centered columns conditionally on <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[G_{j}|X]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[G_{j}|X]=0</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\{1,\ldots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\{1,\ldots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition,</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:15pt;">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The columns of </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are sub-Gaussian, with parameter </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{G}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist
</span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\beta\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\beta\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and
</span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{G}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{G}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that almost surely</span></p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[C_{G}\mid X\right]=\beta C_{X}+\Lambda_{G}(X)\;," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[C_{G}\mid X\right]=\beta C_{X}+\Lambda_{G}(X)\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a positive semi-definite matrix satisfying for some </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa^{-1}\leq\uplambda_{d}(\Lambda_{G}(X))\leq\uplambda_{1}(\Lambda_{G}(X))\leq\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">λ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa^{-1}\leq\uplambda_{d}(\Lambda_{G}(X))\leq\uplambda_{1}(\Lambda_{G}(X))\leq\kappa</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> almost surely on </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{A}_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{A}_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> defined in (</span><a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Part <a href="#S3.I1.i1" title="Item (i) ‣ H 3 (Concentration of 𝐺). ‣ 3 Precision matrix estimation using generic data augmentation ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(i)</span></a> of <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="H 3 (Concentration of 𝐺). ‣ 3 Precision matrix estimation using generic data augmentation ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is a concentration assumption on <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> conditional on <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
similar to <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="H 1 (Concentration of 𝑋). ‣ 2 Inverse covariance estimation using shrinkage method ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Regarding the second part <a href="#S3.I1.i2" title="Item (ii) ‣ H 3 (Concentration of 𝐺). ‣ 3 Precision matrix estimation using generic data augmentation ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(ii)</span></a>, it can be interpreted as a structural assumption.
In most cases, the parameters <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> can be directly derived from the definition of the augmentation process.
<a href="#S3.T1" title="In 3 Precision matrix estimation using generic data augmentation ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provides values of <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> for a range of common DA schemes.
As an example, consider the case where <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn from a TDA procedure of the form <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{j}=g(X_{I_{j}},Z_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{j}=g(X_{I_{j}},Z_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{Z_{j}\}_{j=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{Z_{j}\}_{j=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> are <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">i.i.d.</span> and <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz concentrated (<a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (Lispchitz concentration). ‣ A.2 Discussions on , , ‣ Appendix A In-depth justification of the hypothesis ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>),
and <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I_{j})_{j=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I_{j})_{j=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> are i.i.d, with <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{1}\sim\mathrm{Unif}(\{1,\cdots,n\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{1}\sim\mathrm{Unif}(\{1,\cdots,n\})</annotation></semantics></math>, we also assume that for any <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{Z}\left[g(Z_{1},x)\right]=\sqrt{\beta^{(\mathrm{e})}}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{Z}\left[g(Z_{1},x)\right]=\sqrt{\beta^{(\mathrm{e})}}x</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{(\mathrm{e})}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{(\mathrm{e})}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, it is straightforward to verify that <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="H 3 (Concentration of 𝐺). ‣ 3 Precision matrix estimation using generic data augmentation ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>-<a href="#S3.I1.i2" title="Item (ii) ‣ H 3 (Concentration of 𝐺). ‣ 3 Precision matrix estimation using generic data augmentation ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(ii)</span></a> is satisfied with <math id="S3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\leftarrow\beta^{(\mathrm{e})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\leftarrow\beta^{(\mathrm{e})}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda(X)\leftarrow\Lambda^{(\mathrm{e})}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda(X)\leftarrow\Lambda^{(\mathrm{e})}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> where</p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Lambda^{(\mathrm{e})}(X)=\dfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbb{E}\left[\{g(Z_{1},X_{i})-\sqrt{\beta^{(e)}}X_{i}\}\{g(Z_{1},X_{i})-\sqrt{\beta^{(e)}}X_{i}\}^{\top}\;\middle|\;X_{i}\right]\;." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Lambda^{(\mathrm{e})}(X)=\dfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbb{E}\left[\{g(Z_{1},X_{i})-\sqrt{\beta^{(e)}}X_{i}\}\{g(Z_{1},X_{i})-\sqrt{\beta^{(e)}}X_{i}\}^{\top}\;\middle|\;X_{i}\right]\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.T1" title="In 3 Precision matrix estimation using generic data augmentation ‣ Non-Asymptotic Analysis Of Data Augmentation For Precision Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> below, shows the value of <math id="S3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> for a variety of common data-augmentation scheme.</p>
</div>
